  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This EMPIAR dataset is already showing early scholarly uptake: it has one OpenAlex citation and documented downstream reuse in three records, including journal-article citations. The reuse pattern is small but concrete, and it places the dataset in the path of a high-visibility article in <em>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</em>.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Quantification of gallium cryo-FIB milling damage in biological lamella, released through EMPIAR in 2023 by Lucas BA and Nikolaus Grigorieff, has a modest but clearly traceable impact record so far. The dataset has one indexed citation in OpenAlex, and the citing work is a 2023 article in <em>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</em>, indicating that the resource has already been taken up in a prominent scientific venue.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond the citation count, the record shows downstream reuse in three forms, including journal-article citations and a biomedical article mention. Crossref data-citation records confirm at least two references to the dataset, both in 2023. Taken together, these signals suggest that the dataset is being used as a citable research object rather than only as a background repository item.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape is narrow at present: the captured citing record is concentrated in a single article, associated with advanced electron microscopy techniques and related materials-science concepts. That concentration means the current evidence speaks most strongly to disciplinary relevance within microscopy and specimen-preparation workflows, rather than to broad cross-field diffusion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Public discourse signals are sparse, and the available record does not add social or news attention beyond the scholarly and reuse evidence already observed. Even so, the existing signals are enough to show early adoption: a dataset with one citation, multiple documented reuse events, and placement in a high-profile citation context is beginning to enter the literature as a reusable methodological resource.</p>",
